===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|politics}} A [[government]] where [[power]] is [[share]]d by [[four]] [[people]], ''[[especially]]'' {{lb|en|historical}} the [[w:Herodian tetrarchy|Herodian tetrarchy]] [[establish]]ed in [[Judea]] after the [[death]] of [[w:Herod the Great|Herod]] and the [[w:Tetrarchy|Tetrarchy]] of [[w:Diocletian|Diocletian]] which [[rule]]d the [[Roman Empire]] in the [[year]]s 293-313.
#* '''1996''', Sam Lieu, ''2: Constantine's "Pagan" Vision: The anonymous panegyric on Constantine (310), Pan. Lat. VII(6)'', Samuel N. C. Lieu, Dominic Montserrat (editors), ''From Constantine to Julian: Pagan and Byzantine Views: A Source History'', 2003, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=2ueJAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA66&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEI9wEwLA#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false page 66],
#*: Constantius was not born to the purple and Maximianus was the only original member of the First '''Tetrarchy''' from whom Constantine could satisfactorily derive his rule.
#* '''2010''', Roger Collins, ''Early Medieval Europe, 300-1000'', 3rd Edition, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=ZukcBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA16&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEIggIwLg#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false page 16],
#*: The abdication of Diocletian and Maximian in 305, whether long-planned or just the product of the senior emperor's recent ill health, created a second '''Tetrarchy''', in which the dominant figure should have been Galerius.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2015|author=David M. Gwynn|title=Christianity in the Later Roman Empire: A Sourcebook|chapter=i|page=15|url=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=5aNOBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA15&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrares%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEIiAIwLw#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false|passage=His imperial reorganization saw authority divided between four rulers, the '''Tetrarchy''', to oversee the recovery.}}
# {{lb|en|geography}} The [[land]] [[rule]]d by [[such]] a [[government]], [[either]] [[together]] or [[separately]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1830|author=Edward Greswell|title=Dissertations Upon the Principles and Arrangement of a Harmony of the Gospels|volume=1|chapter=i|page=228|url=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=ggUWAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA229&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrares%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEIbzAS#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false|passage=According to Josephus, Herod Agrippa, who succeeded Antipas in his '''Tetrarchy''', died in the seventh year of his reign;{{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1863|author=James Hewitt|title=Scripture Geography|chapter=i|page=66|url=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=85gEAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA66&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrares%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEIhgMwRg#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false|passage=This district was included with Galilee in the '''tetrarchy''' of Herod Antipas.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1990|author=Seth Schwartz|title=Josephus and Judaean Politics|chapter=i|page=110|url=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=N7MfAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA110&dq=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrares%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiZhKPA5eXOAhWEI5QKHXhZB0sQ6AEI_AEwLQ#v=onepage&q=%22tetrarchy%22%7C%22tetrarchies%22&f=false|passage=After an adventurous career, Aristobulus' son Agrippa was appointed by Gaius, in 38 C.E., king of the former '''tetrarchies''' of Philip and Lysanias and was granted the praetorian ''ornamenta''. Soon after, he was awarded the '''tetrarchy''' of his uncle and brother-in-law Herod Antipas{{...}}.}}

